//
// AN ARGUMENT PAST THE DECLARED PARAMETERS IS REFUSED, AND THE REFUSAL ALONE IS SAID
// (`@FR-F-Arity`, loft#2537).  A call fills every parameter and no more: an argument past the
// declared ones is a compile error.  A compiler-inserted slot is no user parameter, so an extra
// argument never fills a text return's buffer (it once filled `txt`'s and answered `ab` in
// silence) or a record return's `__retbuf`.  And the refused call keeps its callee's type, so the
// code after it is checked against what the call would answer: `rec(1, 2).x` is the arity
// refusal and nothing else — it was followed by "Unknown type null — did you mean 'JNull'?", a
// `null` the program does not contain (loft#2537).  Each callee is named apart, so each
// expectation can only be met by its own refusal, and an error no expectation names fails the
// file.  Too few arguments, and a generic called with too many, are refused one compile pass
// later, which a file with these refusals never reaches: they are
// `a-call-short-of-a-required-parameter-is-refused-refused.loft`.  The Rust twins are
// `tests/issues.rs` `an_argument_past_the_parameters_*`.
//
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/a-call-fills-every-parameter-and-no-more-refused.patch — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/2 -> 0/2 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/2 -> 0/2
//   CHANNEL: the expected errors (each must be met, and nothing else may be reported).
//   ARMED: every run.
//   WITNESS: the control adds `Unknown type null — did you mean 'JNull'?` after the `rec`
//   refusal.
//   HOLDS: the two arity refusals themselves, on both trees.
//
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Too many parameters for txt
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Too many parameters for rec

struct P { x: integer }

fn txt(a: text, b: text = "z") -> text { "{a}{b}" }
fn rec(a: integer) -> P { P { x: a } }

fn past_a_text_buffer() -> text { txt("a", "b", "c") }
fn past_a_record_buffer() -> integer { rec(1, 2).x }
